<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian Riviera</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ligurian Riviera</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian Riviera is the coastal strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between the French border and La Spezia, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> at its centre. It is divided into the Riviera di Ponente to the west and the Riviera di Levante to the east. Its small ports, such as Noli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and Portofino, lived from fishing and seaborne trade, and from the 19th century it became a major resort coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-riviera.jpg" alt="Italian Riviera" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgio Galeotti · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_-_Lerici,_La_Spezia,_Italy_-_August_16,_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Noli, a small port on the western <b>Riviera</b>, freed itself from the Del Carretto marquises and became a self-governing commune in 1192.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-noli">Republic of Noli</a></li></ul></section>
